= Common ground — Technical =

== Possible-world model ==
A Stalnaker-style context set can represent the worlds compatible with what participants mutually accept:

<pre>
C ⊆ W
</pre>

An informative assertion p proposes restricting the context:

<pre>
C' = C ∩ ⟦p⟧
</pre>

This idealization captures information growth but abstracts away many social and cognitive details.

== Common belief and public commitment ==
Mentalist theories model nested beliefs or mutual knowledge.

Commitment-based theories instead track what participants are publicly responsible for in the interaction.

These can diverge when a participant publicly accepts a proposition they privately doubt.

== Grounding ==
Interactional accounts examine how participants establish mutual understanding through acknowledgment, repair, clarification, joint attention, and coordinated action.

Grounding is a process; common ground is often modeled as a state or resource resulting from such processes.

== Dynamic discourse ==
Presupposition, anaphora, reference, questions, and discourse coherence can depend on structured common ground.

Formal systems may enrich C with discourse referents, issue stacks, commitment stores, or probabilistic beliefs.

== Limits ==
Common-ground models can overidealize agreement. Real discourse includes asymmetric knowledge, deception, contested presuppositions, power differences, partial attention, and uncertainty about what others understood.

